%[text] # Open Vocabulary Class Activation Mapping (CAM) via CLIP Surgery
%[text] This example shows how to perform Class Activation Mapping (CAM) in an image over an open vocabulary set using a modified version of the CLIP model.
%[text] CLIP \[[1](internal:M_88cc)\], which stands for Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining, is a multimodal embedding model that embeds images and text into a shared embedding space in which distance between embeddings indicates similarity.  It was originally developed for open vocabulary zero-shot classification. However, the pretrained CLIP model does not give good results for segmentation or explainability tasks.  CLIP Surgery \[[2](internal:M_098d)\] overcomes this limitation by modifying the architecture of CLIP to add a value-only attention branch.  The modified architecture is well suited for the CAM explainability task, in which the input is an image and a prompt, and the output is a heatmap showing where the CLIP model believes there is correlation between the image and the prompt.  This enables downstream open vocabulary tasks, such as semantic segmentation, interactive segmentation, multi-label recognition and multimodal visualization.
%[text] In this example, you generate a CAM from the CLIP network on an image, then apply CLIP Surgery, and finally, generate improved CAM results using the new model.
%[text] ## Instantiate and Format CLIP Model
%[text] Use the VIT-L-14 backbone. The CLIP image encoder returns image embeddings of the CLS token by default. However, to make a CAM, all tokens are required, so remove the final slice layer and reconnect the layers around it.
clipBackbone = "vit-l-14";
clipModel = clipNetwork(clipBackbone, ModelName="CLIP");
clipImageEncoder = removeLayers(clipModel.ImageEncoderNetwork, "slice");
clipImageEncoder = connectLayers(clipImageEncoder, "transformer:resblock24_add2", "lnPost");
clipImageEncoder = initialize(clipImageEncoder);
%%
%[text] ## Display and Preprocess Image
%[text] Preprocess the image using the `preprocessImg` helper function.
I = imread("parkinglot_left.png");
imageshow(I); %[output:8c66be1d]
imgPreprocessed = preprocessImg(I, clipModel.ImageInputSize);
%[text] Set the prompt for which to generate a class activation map.  In this case, we will determine which part of the image is associated with a car.
camPrompt = "car";
%%
%[text] ## Examine CLIP Architecture
%[text] Examine the architecture of CLIP's image encoder, which is a sequential series of transformers having this structure:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:12c4)
%[text] Note that it has 303.9 million parameters.  
analyzeNetwork(clipImageEncoder)
%%
%[text] ## Generate Class Activation Map with CLIP
%[text] Determine which segments of the image are associated with cars.
%[text] The process to generate a Class Activation Map (CAM) is as follows:
%[text] 1. Extract normalized image and text features.  The text features are generated and normalized in the `encodeTextWithPromptEnsemble` helper function.
%[text] 2. Multiply the image and text features.
%[text] 3. Transform the combined features into a CAM.  This is done with the `getSimilarityMap` helper function. \
clipImageFeatures = forward(clipImageEncoder, imgPreprocessed);
clipImageFeatures = clipImageFeatures ./vecnorm(clipImageFeatures);
textFeatures = encodeTextWithPromptEnsemble(clipModel, camPrompt); %[output:996701b1]
clipFeatures = textFeatures' * squeeze(stripdims(clipImageFeatures));
clipCAM = getSimilarityMap(clipFeatures, size(I, [1 2]));
%%
%[text] ## Display Class Activation Map
%[text] Use the `overlayHeatmap` helper function. As we can see, the CLIP CAM heavily favors the background of the image.
overlayHeatmap(I, extractdata(squeeze(clipCAM))); %[output:7394ea56]
title("CAM for Prompt '" + camPrompt + "'"); %[output:7394ea56]
%%
%[text] ## Perform CLIP Surgery
%[text] Apply CLIP Surgery from the paper \[[2](internal:M_098d)\] to the CLIP model. CLIP Surgery adds a dual path to CLIP's vision encoder.  The dual path uses V-V-V attention, meaning that the value parameters are used for the query, key, and value parameters of the attention layer.
%[text] The attached M-file `applyClipSurgery.m` performs the modification.
clipSurgeryImageEncoder = applyCLIPSurgery(clipModel.ImageEncoderNetwork); %[output:234f9ce9]
%[text] After modification, examine the architecture again, which now includes the dual path of V-V-V attention layers, shown on the right in the image below, which captures the last transformer and dual path in the network.  The original path is on the left.  The original transformer layer has been split into key, query, and value linear layers followed by the transformer's output projection.  The value layer is the only input to the V-V-V attention layer of the dual path.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:741b)
%[text] The highlighted layer shows the new V-V-V attention layer, and directly to its left is the original attention layer which feeds into the preexisting branch.
analyzeNetwork(clipSurgeryImageEncoder)
%[text] The number of parameters has not changed because parameters are reused.
%%
%[text] ## Generate Class Activation Map with Modified CLIP Network
%[text] Now that CLIP surgery has been applied, generate a new CAM.  The text features do not change, only the image features need to be regenerated.  Use the `clipFeatureSurgery` helper function to combine the text features with the image features.
surgeryImageFeatures = forward(clipSurgeryImageEncoder, imgPreprocessed);
surgeryImageFeatures = surgeryImageFeatures ./vecnorm(surgeryImageFeatures);
surgeryFeatures = clipFeatureSurgery(surgeryImageFeatures, textFeatures);
surgeryCAM = getSimilarityMap(surgeryFeatures, size(I, [1, 2]));
%%
%[text] ## Display Improved Class Activation Map
%[text] Use the `overlayHeatmap` helper function.  Note that the heatmap is much improved after applying CLIP Surgery.
overlayHeatmap(I, extractdata(squeeze(surgeryCAM))); %[output:86f4c40d]
title("CAM after CLIP Surgery for Prompt '" + camPrompt + "'"); %[output:86f4c40d]
%%
%[text] ## Helper Functions
function Iresized = preprocessImg(I,targetSize)
    arguments
        I (:, :, :, :) % H-by-W-by-C-by-B numeric array
        targetSize (1, 2) double % row and column dimensions of output image
    end
    
    if isdlarray(I)
        I = extractdata(I);
    end
    
    % Convert from grayscale to RGB.
    if(size(I,3)==1)
        I = repmat(I,[1 1 3]);
    end
    
    % Resize.
    scale = targetSize(1)/min(size(I,1),size(I,2));
    Iresized = imresize(I,scale,"bicubic");
    
    % Center crop.
    crop = centerCropWindow3d(size(Iresized),[targetSize size(I,3)]);
    Iresized = imcrop3(Iresized,crop);
    
    % Cast.
    Iresized = dlarray(double(Iresized), "SSCB");
end
%%
function textFeatures = encodeTextWithPromptEnsemble(model, objectsText)
    arguments
        model (1, 1) clipNetwork
        objectsText (1, :) string
    end

    % Use 85 default prompts for ImageNet, as per the CLIP Surgery paper.
    promptTemplate = [ ...
        "a bad photo of a %s.", ...
        "a photo of many %s.", ...
        "a sculpture of a %s.", ...
        "a photo of the hard to see %s.", ...
        "a low resolution photo of the %s.", ...
        "a rendering of a %s.", ...
        "graffiti of a %s.", ...
        "a bad photo of the %s.", ...
        "a cropped photo of the %s.", ...
        "a tattoo of a %s.", ...
        "the embroidered %s.", ...
        "a photo of a hard to see %s.", ...
        "a bright photo of a %s.", ...
        "a photo of a clean %s.", ...
        "a photo of a dirty %s.", ...
        "a dark photo of the %s.", ...
        "a drawing of a %s.", ...
        "a photo of my %s.", ...
        "the plastic %s.", ...
        "a photo of the cool %s.", ...
        "a close-up photo of a %s.", ...
        "a black and white photo of the %s.", ...
        "a painting of the %s.", ...
        "a painting of a %s.", ...
        "a pixelated photo of the %s.", ...
        "a sculpture of the %s.", ...
        "a bright photo of the %s.", ...
        "a cropped photo of a %s.", ...
        "a plastic %s.", ...
        "a photo of the dirty %s.", ...
        "a jpeg corrupted photo of a %s.", ...
        "a blurry photo of the %s.", ...
        "a photo of the %s.", ...
        "a good photo of the %s.", ...
        "a rendering of the %s.", ...
        "a %s in a video game.", ...
        "a photo of one %s.", ...
        "a doodle of a %s.", ...
        "a close-up photo of the %s.", ...
        "a photo of a %s.", ...
        "the origami %s.", ...
        "the %s in a video game.", ...
        "a sketch of a %s.", ...
        "a doodle of the %s.", ...
        "a origami %s.", ...
        "a low resolution photo of a %s.", ...
        "the toy %s.", ...
        "a rendition of the %s.", ...
        "a photo of the clean %s.", ...
        "a photo of a large %s.", ...
        "a rendition of a %s.", ...
        "a photo of a nice %s.", ...
        "a photo of a weird %s.", ...
        "a blurry photo of a %s.", ...
        "a cartoon %s.", ...
        "art of a %s.", ...
        "a sketch of the %s.", ...
        "a embroidered %s.", ...
        "a pixelated photo of a %s.", ...
        "itap of the %s.", ...
        "a jpeg corrupted photo of the %s.", ...
        "a good photo of a %s.", ...
        "a plushie %s.", ...
        "a photo of the nice %s.", ...
        "a photo of the small %s.", ...
        "a photo of the weird %s.", ...
        "the cartoon %s.", ...
        "art of the %s.", ...
        "a drawing of the %s.", ...
        "a photo of the large %s.", ...
        "a black and white photo of a %s.", ...
        "the plushie %s.", ...
        "a dark photo of a %s.", ...
        "itap of a %s.", ...
        "graffiti of the %s.", ...
        "a toy %s.", ...
        "itap of my %s.", ...
        "a photo of a cool %s.", ...
        "a photo of a small %s.", ...
        "a tattoo of the %s.", ...
        "there is a %s in the scene.", ...
        "there is the %s in the scene.", ...
        "this is a %s in the scene.", ...
        "this is the %s in the scene.", ...
        "this is one %s in the scene." ...
        ];

    numObjects = numel(objectsText);
    numPrompts = numel(promptTemplate);

    % Generate all prompts for all objects.
    allPrompts = strings(1, numObjects * numPrompts);
    for i = 1:numObjects
        idx = (i-1)*numPrompts + (1:numPrompts);
        allPrompts(idx) = replace(promptTemplate, "%s", objectsText(i));
    end

    disp("Running batched inference on text encoder for " + numel(allPrompts) + " text prompts...");
    allEmbeddings = model.extractTextEmbeddings(allPrompts);
    disp("Done.");

    allEmbeddings = allEmbeddings ./ vecnorm(allEmbeddings, 2, 1);

    % Reshape to [embeddingLength, numPrompts, numObjects].
    allEmbeddings = reshape(allEmbeddings, model.EmbeddingLength, numPrompts, numObjects);

    % Average over prompts.
    textFeatures = mean(allEmbeddings, 2);
    textFeatures = squeeze(textFeatures);

    % Normalize features.
    textFeatures = textFeatures ./ vecnorm(textFeatures, 2, 1);
end
%%
function sim = clipFeatureSurgery(imageFeatures, textFeatures)
    arguments
        imageFeatures (768, :, 257) dlarray
        textFeatures (768, :) dlarray
    end

    % Multiply CLS image embedding by text features.
    prob = stripdims(squeeze(imageFeatures(:, :, 1)))' * textFeatures;
    prob = softmax(prob*2, DataFormat="BC");
    
    % Weights to restrain influence of obvious classes on others.
    w = prob ./ mean(prob, 2);
    [c, b, ni] = size(imageFeatures);
    nt = size(textFeatures, 2);
    
    % Reshape for broadcasting.
    imageFeatures = permute(stripdims(imageFeatures), [2 3 1]);
    imageFeatures = reshape(imageFeatures, [b, ni, 1, c]);
    textFeatures = stripdims(textFeatures)';
    textFeatures = reshape(textFeatures, [1, 1, nt, c]);
    
    % Determine correlation between prompts and image.
    feats = imageFeatures .* textFeatures;
    feats = feats .* reshape(w, [1, 1, nt, 1]);
    
    if nt > 1
        % Remove redundant features.
        redundantFeats = mean(feats, 3);
        feats = feats - redundantFeats;
    end
    sim = sum(feats, 4);
end
%%
function similarityMap = getSimilarityMap(similarity, imgSize)
    arguments
        similarity dlarray
        imgSize (1, 2) double {mustBeInteger, mustBePositive, mustBeFinite}
    end

    % Remove CLS embedding.
    similarity = similarity(:, 2:end, :);

    % Min-max norm.
    similarityMin = min(similarity, [], 2);
    similarityMax = max(similarity, [], 2);
    similarityNorm = (similarity - similarityMin) ./ (similarityMax - similarityMin);

    % Reshape.
    side = round(sqrt(size(similarity, 2)));
    similarityReshaped = reshape(similarityNorm, [1, side, side, size(similarity, 3)]);

    % Interpolate.
    similarityPermuted = permute(similarityReshaped, [1 4 3 2]);
    similarityMap = dlresize(similarityPermuted, OutputSize=imgSize, Method="linear", DataFormat="BCSS");
    similarityMap = permute(similarityMap, [1 3 4 2]);
end
%%
function overlayHeatmap(I, heatmap)
    heatmap = rescale(heatmap);
    heatmapRGB = ind2rgb(uint8(heatmap * 255), jet(256));
    imshow(I);
    hold on
    h = imshow(heatmapRGB);
    h.AlphaData = 0.5;
    hold off
end
%%
